      "problem": "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $T$ has proportional rows, and yet $T$ is not the zero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ and $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line that passes through the origin.\n\nMoreover, the line must pass through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point $(2,2),$ which is defined by the (repeated) columns of the matrix $T.$ \n\nComputing the slope $m$ of this line, we get\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{2-0}{2-0}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=x.$",
